1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,290
A startup’s financials help you
understand what it has earned,

2
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:05,812
spent, and still owes.

3
00:00:05,812 --> 00:00:10,443
Revenue is not the same as profit,
and cash on hand is not the same

4
00:00:10,443 --> 00:00:11,825
as available runway.

5
00:00:11,825 --> 00:00:15,500
Burn rate describes cash being
used over time.

6
00:00:15,500 --> 00:00:19,354
Gross burn looks at cash
operating outflows; net burn

7
00:00:19,354 --> 00:00:22,262
subtracts cash inflows such as
receipts.

8
00:00:22,262 --> 00:00:26,147
Runway is a rough estimate of
how long current cash might last

9
00:00:26,147 --> 00:00:27,650
at the current net burn.

10
00:00:27,650 --> 00:00:33,233
In a fictional example, a company
has $180,000 in cash, spends

11
00:00:33,233 --> 00:00:38,725
$60,000 each month, and collects
$30,000 in monthly receipts.

12
00:00:38,725 --> 00:00:44,288
Its net burn is $30,000 per month,
so simple runway is six months.

13
00:00:44,288 --> 00:00:48,790
That assumes the pace stays
constant and ignores timing, debt,

14
00:00:48,790 --> 00:00:50,388
and one-time expenses.

15
00:00:50,388 --> 00:00:54,047
Check the period covered by
statements, unpaid bills,

16
00:00:54,047 --> 00:00:57,638
customer concentration, and
planned use of proceeds.

17
00:00:57,638 --> 00:01:01,559
A runway estimate is a snapshot,
not a promise that the company

18
00:01:01,559 --> 00:01:04,920
will raise more money or survive
until the date shown.

